    "seconds": 33,
    "summary": "The anomaly is mitigated: the `geo` service is running again and all 19 pods in `test-hotel-reservation` are Running and ready.\n\n**What I found**\n- `deploy/geo` was crash-looping (0/1, `Error`/`BackOff`).\n- Its logs showed `not authorized on geo-db` when it queried `mongodb-geo`.\n- The `failure-admin-geo` configmap contains a `revoke-admin-geo-mongo.sh` script that removes `readWrite` on `geo-db` from the `admin` user, which geo authenticates as. That is the injected fault.\n- Live check: `admin` had only `userAdminAnyDatabase` on `admin`, with no `geo-db` role.\n\n**What I changed**\n- Ran the grant from the configmap's `revoke-mitigate-admin-geo-mongo.sh` against the live `mongodb-geo` pod: `db.grantRolesToUser('admin', [{role: 'readWrite', db: 'geo-db'}])`. `admin` now has `readWrite` on `geo-db` again.\n- The geo pod restarted, connected to MongoDB, and registered with Consul.\n\n**Not changed**\n- The `failure-admin-geo` configmap and the revoke script are still in the cluster. If the fault is re-injected, geo will fail again. I left them alone because removing them was not part of the fix.\n- I did not change any other deployment or the root user.",
